<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Google Developers Blog</title><link>https://developers.googleblog.com/rss/</link><description>Updates on changes and additions to the Google Developers Blog.</description><atom:link href="https://developers.googleblog.com/feeds/posts/default/" rel="self"/><language>en-us</language><lastBuildDate>Wed, 18 Nov 2026 05:02:17 +0000</lastBuildDate><item><title>Accelerating development for massive Git repositories</title><link>https://developers.googleblog.com/accelerating-development-for-massive-git-repositories/</link><description>Meet Another Build File Service (ABFS), Google's open-source content-addressable filesystem and build cache that cuts Android checkouts to &lt;8s and builds to ~2m.</description><guid>https://developers.googleblog.com/accelerating-development-for-massive-git-repositories/</guid></item><item><title>Gemini 4 Hydrogen: a faster model for general purpose robotics</title><link>https://developers.googleblog.com/gemini-hydrogen-for-robotics/</link><description>Google's Gemini 4 Hydrogen brings strong spatial and embodied reasoning capabilities to a fast, efficient model built specifically for robotics. To help developers put these capabilities to work, the new Robotics plugin for Google Antigravity transforms the platform into a comprehensive workbench supporting rapid hardware bring-up, MuJoCo simulation, and closed-loop control. Developers can leverage these tools to transition from an unboxed robot arm to a fully functional real-to-sim-to-real application in an afternoon using natural language prompts and day-one support in the google-genai SDKs.</description><guid>https://developers.googleblog.com/gemini-hydrogen-for-robotics/</guid></item><item><title>Build full-stack Android apps with Gemini in Google AI Studio</title><link>https://developers.googleblog.com/build-full-stack-android-apps-with-gemini-in-google-ai-studio/</link><description>Google AI Studio now lets developers build, test, and ship full-stack Kotlin Android apps from a single prompt—complete with Cloud Firestore, Firebase Authentication, Firebase AI Logic, a faster HD emulator with audio, visual screenshot debugging, and direct publishing to your personal Google Play library.</description><guid>https://developers.googleblog.com/build-full-stack-android-apps-with-gemini-in-google-ai-studio/</guid></item><item><title>Agents are sessions, not requests: size GKE node pools for AI agent sessions</title><link>https://developers.googleblog.com/agents-are-sessions-not-requests-size-gke-node-pools-for-ai-agent-sessions/</link><description>AI agent sessions remain active and idle for most of the workday, making traditional one-Pod-per-session allocations inefficient for cluster resources. By using open-source tools like Agent Substrate and AX on GKE, developers can park idle sessions in object storage to release compute resources while maintaining session state. This approach optimizes resource utilization and reduces Kubernetes node costs by up to 40%.</description><guid>https://developers.googleblog.com/agents-are-sessions-not-requests-size-gke-node-pools-for-ai-agent-sessions/</guid></item><item><title>TPU Model Design Considerations and Optimization Guide</title><link>https://developers.googleblog.com/tpu-model-design-considerations-and-optimization-guide/</link><description>The newly launched AI-Hypercomputer/tpu-codesign guide provides hardware-grounded design rules to help developers maximize performance on TPUs for LLMs and Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) workloads. Key principles include aligning reduction and output dimensions to 256 for systolic arrays, adopting LatentMoE to compress routed token traffic, and mapping parallel axes directly onto physical links.</description><guid>https://developers.googleblog.com/tpu-model-design-considerations-and-optimization-guide/</guid></item><item><title>Unlocking Stable On-Policy RL on TPU with Bitwise-Zero Train–Inference Mismatch</title><link>https://developers.googleblog.com/unlocking-stable-on-policy-rl-on-tpu-with-bitwise-zero-traininference-mismatch/</link><description>How we eliminated floating-point train–inference logprob drift for on-policy LLM reinforcement learning across 64 Cloud TPU v5p chips using a generalizable four-part JAX and Pallas recipe in Tunix.</description><guid>https://developers.googleblog.com/unlocking-stable-on-policy-rl-on-tpu-with-bitwise-zero-traininference-mismatch/</guid></item><item><title>Securing Your Agents: A Hands-On Guide to Agent Anomaly Detection</title><link>https://developers.googleblog.com/securing-your-agents-a-hands-on-guide-to-agent-anomaly-detection/</link><description>This guide walks developers through setting up Agent Anomaly Detection on the Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform to monitor AI agent telemetry and catch behavioral risks. It demonstrates how to deploy a sample customer support agent, trigger out-of-scope security findings, and configure proactive alerts using Cloud Monitoring and Security Command Center.</description><guid>https://developers.googleblog.com/securing-your-agents-a-hands-on-guide-to-agent-anomaly-detection/</guid></item><item><title>Catch Go bugs that fuzzing misses with Gemini and a constraint solver</title><link>https://developers.googleblog.com/catch-go-bugs-that-fuzzing-misses-with-gemini-and-a-constraint-solver/</link><description>How pairing Go's surviving compiler bounds-check traps with Gemini 4 Argon and the Z3 constraint solver catches latent out-of-bounds panics in Go code and uncovers a slice-capacity blind spot in go test -fuzz.</description><guid>https://developers.googleblog.com/catch-go-bugs-that-fuzzing-misses-with-gemini-and-a-constraint-solver/</guid></item><item><title>How Agent Anomaly Detection scores on benchmarks we didn't write</title><link>https://developers.googleblog.com/how-agent-anomaly-detection-scores-on-benchmarks-we-didnt-write/</link><description>Agent Anomaly Detection (AAD) provides asynchronous, low-latency monitoring for AI agents by analyzing OpenTelemetry traces and logs to flag goal hijacks and tool misuse. Tested across five public security benchmarks, AAD successfully caught 87% of harmful instructions carried out by agents without adding runtime latency. Developers can integrate AAD alongside semantic governance and strict tool limits as part of a defense-in-depth strategy to secure agentic applications.</description><guid>https://developers.googleblog.com/how-agent-anomaly-detection-scores-on-benchmarks-we-didnt-write/</guid></item><item><title>A2UI 1.0: Build delightful, fast, reliable, agentic experiences</title><link>https://developers.googleblog.com/a2ui-10-build-delightful-fast-reliable-agentic-experiences/</link><description>A2UI 1.0 is an open protocol for generative UI that enables AI agents to render interactive, native screens across web, mobile, and desktop applications. Developers can leverage open-source renderer SDKs—including React, Angular, Lit, Flutter, SwiftUI, and Jetpack Compose—alongside agent SDKs for Python, TypeScript, and Dart. The release introduces bidirectional function calls, swappable inference formats, and secure data binding to help teams seamlessly integrate generative UI into their product stacks.</description><guid>https://developers.googleblog.com/a2ui-10-build-delightful-fast-reliable-agentic-experiences/</guid></item><item><title>On-Device Speech Recognition and Text-to-Speech in Google AI Edge Gallery</title><link>https://developers.googleblog.com/on-device-speech-recognition-and-text-to-speech-in-google-ai-edge-gallery/</link><description>Google AI Edge Gallery now supports on-device, offline Automatic Speech Recognition (ASR) and Text-to-Speech (TTS) capabilities for building developer-friendly voice applications. Developers can leverage ML Kit for optimized Android performance, or use MediaPipe Tasks and LiteRT for cross-platform open-weight model integration. This local pipeline seamlessly pairs lightweight speech models with on-device small language models like Gemini Nano and Gemma 4 to enable real-time streaming transcription and low-latency synthesis.</description><guid>https://developers.googleblog.com/on-device-speech-recognition-and-text-to-speech-in-google-ai-edge-gallery/</guid></item><item><title>Announcing Long-Context Training on TPUs with MaxText Context Parallelism</title><link>https://developers.googleblog.com/announcing-long-context-training-on-tpus-with-maxtext-context-parallelism/</link><description>Google Cloud's MaxText framework now supports long-context training up to 14 million tokens on Cloud TPUs by implementing four context-parallel strategies: All-gather, A2A, P2P, and USP. Powered by the Tokamax Splash Attention kernel, these strategies effectively manage attention compute and memory bottlenecks without materializing full key-value sequences in high-bandwidth memory. Developers can adopt these capabilities in MaxText v0.2.4 to achieve high Model FLOPs Utilization (MFU) across large-scale model training workflows.</description><guid>https://developers.googleblog.com/announcing-long-context-training-on-tpus-with-maxtext-context-parallelism/</guid></item><item><title>From prompt to app: Build with Google Maps in AI Studio</title><link>https://developers.googleblog.com/from-prompt-to-app-build-with-google-maps-in-ai-studio/</link><description>Developers can now build applications powered by Google Maps Platform directly within Google AI Studio by prompting Gemini with real-world geospatial concepts. The integration eliminates traditional configuration hurdles by providing one-click demo keys that generate instantly without requiring Cloud Console redirects or credit cards. This setup grants developers immediate access to core APIs—including Maps, Places, and Routes—supported by daily sandbox quotas for seamless prototyping.</description><guid>https://developers.googleblog.com/from-prompt-to-app-build-with-google-maps-in-ai-studio/</guid></item><item><title>What is Pathways? Single-controller training for large models</title><link>https://developers.googleblog.com/what-is-pathways-single-controller-training-for-large-models/</link><description>Pathways is a single-controller distributed training system designed to coordinate large-scale model training across multiple accelerator groups by representing computations as a global dataflow graph. Unlike traditional SPMD models like DDP and FSDP that rely on synchronous collectives, Pathways uses asynchronous dispatch and a central scheduler to maximize hardware utilization and prevent deadlocks in complex workloads. By decoupling model definition from physical hardware via virtual devices, developers can efficiently scale complex training architectures and seamlessly handle dynamic workloads like reinforcement learning post-training.</description><guid>https://developers.googleblog.com/what-is-pathways-single-controller-training-for-large-models/</guid></item><item><title>Agent Distraction</title><link>https://developers.googleblog.com/agent-distraction/</link><description>Analysis of over 913,000 coding agent sessions reveals that 13% expose agents to inline TODO(b/...) comments. In 6% of encounters, agents act on these comments, with 83% being entirely unprompted by the developer. These detours result in significant overhead—adding 70% more steps, 77% more tokens, and 105% more wait time per session—split between chasing closed tickets (41%) and unauthorized scope creep on open bugs (57%). Higher-capability models show higher distraction rates, highlighting the need for automated cleanup and linters to maintain agent-friendly codebases</description><guid>https://developers.googleblog.com/agent-distraction/</guid></item><item><title>Cost per task: Using cheap classifiers to route across Gemini models and thinking levels</title><link>https://developers.googleblog.com/cost-per-task-using-cheap-classifiers-to-route-across-gemini-models-and-thinking-levels/</link><description>Per-token API prices keep falling, yet engineering bills for autonomous coding agents keep climbing....</description><guid>https://developers.googleblog.com/cost-per-task-using-cheap-classifiers-to-route-across-gemini-models-and-thinking-levels/</guid></item><item><title>DevFest is back!</title><link>https://developers.googleblog.com/devfest-is-back/</link><description>DevFest 2026 runs from October 1 to December 31, bringing together nearly a million developers across more than 800 global events centered on the agentic AI era. Under the theme "Build, Secure, Scale," participants gain hands-on experience with Google’s developer stack—including Gemini, Google AI Studio, Google Antigravity, and Web MCP—through live workshops and codelabs. The conference focuses on enabling developers to prototype rapidly, ensure secure and private deployments, and leverage production-ready infrastructure.</description><guid>https://developers.googleblog.com/devfest-is-back/</guid></item><item><title>Build Generative UI Apps with A2UI &amp; CopilotKit Agents</title><link>https://developers.googleblog.com/build-generative-ui-apps-with-a2ui-copilotkit-agents/</link><description>See Google A2Ui in action! Learn how to natively render agentic UIs across Langchain, AWS Strands, Microsoft agents, and Google ADK using CopilotKit SDKs for A2UI.</description><guid>https://developers.googleblog.com/build-generative-ui-apps-with-a2ui-copilotkit-agents/</guid></item><item><title>Hill-Climbing Agent Quality with Agents CLI Evaluation</title><link>https://developers.googleblog.com/hill-climbing-agent-quality-with-agents-cli-evaluation/</link><description>This post walks through the Agents CLI evaluation loop for systematically improving agent performance through continuous testing and grading. It demonstrates how to catch prompt regressions, evaluate model backend swaps using side-by-side trace comparisons, and automate evaluation passes. Developers will also learn how to integrate nightly scheduled runs with threshold-based alerting to track quality over time.</description><guid>https://developers.googleblog.com/hill-climbing-agent-quality-with-agents-cli-evaluation/</guid></item><item><title>Smaller apps and faster GPU inference with LiteRT in Google Play services</title><link>https://developers.googleblog.com/smaller-apps-and-faster-gpu-inference-with-litert-in-google-play-services/</link><description>LiteRT in Google Play services now offers GPU inference powered by ML Drift, delivering about 1.4x faster performance alongside the new CompiledModel API. Switching from bundled binaries reduces app size by approximately 8 MB while enabling seamless, automatic runtime updates across billions of devices. Developers can easily adopt these updates by updating their dependencies and utilizing the new Android Studio LiteRT skill for automated migration.</description><guid>https://developers.googleblog.com/smaller-apps-and-faster-gpu-inference-with-litert-in-google-play-services/</guid></item></channel></rss>